      "On the night before His crucifixion, Jesus did something only a slave or a woman would have done. He took off His outer garment, wrapped a towel around His waist, knelt before His disciples, and washed their feet. The Lord of glory washed the feet of the men who would, within hours, abandon Him. This is the picture the Church must never lose. Service in Christ's name is not a strategy. It is a posture. It is a towel.",
      "Service has many forms. The hidden kind: doing the dishes for someone tired, sitting with a dying neighbour, picking up trash no one else will. The structural kind: serving the poor, the sick, the prisoner, the foreigner — Matthew 25 names these as the test of true faith. The body-of-Christ kind: serving the local church through teaching, cleaning, hospitality, ushering, counseling, hosting the prayer meeting. None is more spiritual than the others. All are received by the King.",
      "Several pitfalls. Service can become a hiding place — a way of avoiding intimacy with God or others by always being the helper. Service can become a performance — a way of building reputation. Service can become resentment — done while keeping score. The cure is to keep service small, hidden, and rooted in prayer. Do something today for someone who can never repay you. Tell no one. Watch what God does in your own heart.",
